## Summary of the changes

The fork adds Sixel as a second pane graphics transport so that pane programs
which emit Sixel (file-manager previewers such as `lf` with a `chafa` preview
script, `img2sixel`, OpenTUI-backed agents) render on Sixel-only outer
terminals such as `foot`.

The changes are:

1. Sixel decode from pane output, including band parameters, private-set
   parameters, DECGRA/DECSDM handling, and string terminators (`ESC \` and ST),
   without treating a lone `~` as a terminator.
2. Sixel encoding for the outer terminal: per-frame palette over the 216
   register budget, full-width band lines, DECGRA PAD offsets, and uniform
   scaling that preserves the source aspect ratio.
3. Relay sizing from the cell pitch the host terminal reports, bounded by a
   target pixel budget and a payload ceiling. A cell pitch reported as zero is
   treated as unknown and never fabricated toward the pane program.
4. Placement: relayed frames paint on the cells that the placement names, and
   are re-emitted behind pane text that overwrites their cells.
5. Pane capability advertisement: the pane answers Device Attributes with the
   Sixel feature, which is what makes Sixel-speaking programs emit Sixel inside
   a pane instead of falling back to protocols the outer terminal cannot show.
6. Pacman/AUR installs are recognised as package-manager-managed installs, so
   `herdr update` refuses to overwrite a packaged build with an upstream binary
   and prints the package-manager update instruction instead.

